/**
# Raw Arguments (argv)

Raw argument passthrough with `param (*argv)`.

A script whose params are exactly one variadic positional param and no named
params opts into RAW argument handling: every CLI argument is passed straight
through as a string (no `--name` parsing), and argv[0] is the module path used
to invoke it — like a C program's argv.

Run with:
    gad samples/32_raw_argv.gad build --target=wasm -- --verbose
    # argv == ["samples/32_raw_argv.gad", "build", "--target=wasm", "--verbose"]

As the main module, a single bare `--` options terminator is dropped (it only
separates gad's own flags from your argv). Contrast `param (*args, sep=",")`,
where `--sep` would be parsed as a named argument.

See doc/getting-started.md (Raw argument passthrough) for details.
**/

param (*argv)

println("program:", argv[0])
println("args:   ", argv[1:])
println("count:  ", len(argv))

return argv
